    "body": "Nearly half of supply chain and planning professionals say their technology is more ready for AI than their rules for overseeing it. Forty-eight percent rated their technology platforms higher than their AI governance, which covers decisions such as when a tool can act on its own and when someone needs to sign off, according to a new survey by RELEX Solutions.\nWhat’s Related\n“Technology isn’t the primary limitation to becoming an AI-ready company,” RELEX said in the report.\nGovernance had the lowest average score of the five areas RELEX looked at, at 4.4 out of 10. It ranked last or tied for last for 58% of respondents. Yet just 15% mentioned governance, risk, or security when describing their AI goals.\nLaurence Brenig-Jones, vice president of product at RELEX, gave an example: “When AI recommends buying more inventory, acting on that recommendation commits cash.” He said finance, planning, and operations teams should agree up front on what the system can do, which decisions need approval, and what results would justify giving it more authority.\nThe data comes from the RELEX State of Supply Chain AI Practitioner Pulse, based on intake surveys from 108 registrants at 80 organizations across retail , manufacturing , consumer packaged goods , and distribution. They came from five groups in the company’s 2026 AI Masterclass Series and rated their companies on a 10-point scale. RELEX said the sample was self-selected and the results “are not representative of the industries as a whole.”\nMost companies gave themselves poor scores\nOverall AI readiness averaged 4.9 out of 10, and only 8% of respondents rated their organizations 7 or higher. Planning processes and change leadership tied for the highest average at 5.1, while data and technology platforms each averaged 4.9.\nWhen asked about their AI goals, 32% mentioned finding practical uses or figuring out where to start, and 30% mentioned building a strategy or roadmap. One in five brought up measuring return on investment . RELEX counted more than one theme in an answer when it applied.\nRetail leads, governance comes last\nRetail respondents averaged 5.3 for overall readiness, compared with 4.5 for manufacturing and CPG. The widest gaps were in planning processes (5.8 vs. 4.6) and technology platforms (5.4 vs. 4.3). Governance came last for both groups, at 4.7 for retail and 4.1 for manufacturing and CPG.\nRELEX recommended starting with decisions that are made often, tie directly to a measurable financial impact, and have enough data to track progress. The company also suggested reviewing AI performance, exceptions, and manual overrides weekly, then giving the tool more room to act as trust and results improve.",
